👉 design the invoke protocol + packet format (this is the backbone)

Perfect—this is the spine of everything. We’ll define a binary, QUIC-native, identity-first invoke protocol that replaces HTTP entirely.

I’ll keep it implementable: message types, headers, flows, and wire format.

🧠 0. Goals (why this protocol exists)
No URLs, no verbs → invoke(function)

No implicit trust → identity on every call

No polling → streaming first

Low latency → binary, multiplexed over QUIC

Works across fabrics → federation-ready

🧩 1. Naming
Protocol: IFP — InterFabric Protocol

Transport: QUIC (mandatory), mTLS (mandatory)

Unit of work: Invocation

⚙️ 2. Connection Model
Client Runtime ── QUIC+mTLS ── Fabric Node
                  (multiplexed streams)
One connection → many concurrent invocations (streams)

0-RTT resumption where safe

Outbound-first (NAT friendly)

🧱 3. Framing Model
Each QUIC stream carries a message sequence:

[ INVOKE ] → [ DATA … ] → [ RESULT | ERROR ] → [ END ]
All messages share a common header + typed body.

🧾 4. Common Header (fixed + varint fields)
0               1               2               3
+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+
| Ver | Type    | Flags         | HeaderLen (varint)            |
+---------------+---------------+---------------+---------------+
| InvocationID (16 bytes UUID)                                 |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| TraceID (16 bytes)                                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| Timestamp (varint, µs since epoch)                           |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| BodyLen (varint)                                             |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
| ...extensions (TLV, optional)                                |
+--------------------------------------------------------------+
Fields
Ver: protocol version (e.g., 1)

Type: message type (see below)

Flags: streaming, compressed, end-of-stream, etc.

InvocationID: correlates the entire call

TraceID: for end-to-end tracing (console fabric)

Extensions (TLV): forward-compatible metadata

🔢 5. Message Types
Type	Name	Direction	Purpose
0x01	INVOKE	C → F	Start invocation
0x02	DATA	C ↔ F	Stream request/response chunks
0x03	RESULT	F → C	Final success payload
0x04	ERROR	F → C	Failure payload
0x05	CANCEL	C → F	Cancel invocation
0x06	HEARTBEAT	↔	Keepalive
0x07	POLICY_HINT	↔	Optional policy context
0x08	FEDERATION	↔	Cross-fabric envelope
(C = Client, F = Fabric)

📨 6. INVOKE Message (core)
Body (compact binary; CBOR or protobuf-like)
{
  "function": "auth.login",
  "version": "v1",
  "args": { "user": "john", "pass": "..." },
  "caller": {
    "id": "frontend.app",
    "fabric": "fabric://veer.prod.india"
  },
  "capabilities": ["auth.invoke"],
  "deadline_ms": 5000,
  "priority": "P1",
  "target": {
    "fabric": "fabric://local",
    "constraints": {
      "region": "apac",
      "latency_ms": "<50"
    }
  }
}
Notes
function replaces URL + method

caller is identity (signed—see §8)

target is hint, fabric decides placement

🔄 7. DATA Message (streaming)
Used for:

large inputs (upload)

streaming outputs (subscriptions)

{
  "seq": 3,
  "chunk": <bytes>,
  "eof": false
}
Ordered per stream

Backpressure via QUIC flow control

✅ 8. RESULT Message
{
  "status": "OK",
  "result": { ... },
  "metrics": {
    "latency_ms": 12,
    "node": "edge-12"
  }
}
Small results inline

Large results continue via DATA frames

❌ 9. ERROR Message
{
  "code": "POLICY_DENIED",
  "message": "caller not authorized",
  "retryable": false,
  "details": { ... }
}
Common codes:

POLICY_DENIED

NOT_FOUND

TIMEOUT

RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED

FABRIC_UNAVAILABLE

🔐 10. Identity & Signing (mandatory)
Every INVOKE carries a detached signature over:

hash( header + body )
Identity Block (in extensions TLV)
{
  "sig_alg": "ed25519",
  "signature": "<bytes>",
  "cert_chain": ["leaf", "intermediate"],
  "claims": {
    "sub": "frontend.app",
    "fabric": "veer.prod.india",
    "exp": 1710000000,
    "caps": ["auth.invoke"]
  }
}
Verified before execution

Short-lived certs (minutes)

🛡️ 11. Policy Evaluation Hook
At fabric entry:

verify_signature()
→ evaluate_policy(claims, function, args)
→ allow | deny
Optional POLICY_HINT message can be returned early for UX (e.g., missing capability).

🌐 12. Federation Envelope (cross-fabric)
When target.fabric != local:

Type: FEDERATION

{
  "outer": {
    "source_fabric": "veer.prod.india",
    "target_fabric": "partner.analytics.eu"
  },
  "inner_invoke": { ...original INVOKE... },
  "scoped_identity": {
    "issuer": "veer.prod.india",
    "token": "<signed, short-lived>",
    "claims": { "role": "analytics-client" }
  }
}
Fabric B trusts issuer A, not raw caller

Double policy check (egress + ingress)

⏱️ 13. Deadlines, Retries, Idempotency
deadline_ms is required

idempotency_key (optional TLV) for safe retries

Client retries on:

TIMEOUT (if idempotent)

FABRIC_UNAVAILABLE

⚡ 14. Priorities & QoS
Header Flags/Priority influence scheduling:

P0: panic / control

P1: user actions

P2: background

P3: debug

Fabric can:

preempt

shed load (drop P3 first)

🔁 15. Subscriptions (streaming pattern)
INVOKE (subscribe)
→ continuous DATA (events)
→ END when cancelled
Example:

{ "function": "orders.stream", "args": { "region": "apac" } }
🧰 16. Compression & Encoding
Body encoding: CBOR (default), optional protobuf

Compression flag:

none (default)

zstd (for large DATA)

🔍 17. Observability (built-in)
Every message carries:

TraceID

optional SpanID (extension)

Fabric emits Console events automatically:

{ "type": "trace", "invocation": "...", "latency": 12 }
